WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:08.140
Congratulations! You've passed a one percent treaty. Now you've got twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year flowing

2
00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:14.140
into the one percent Treaty Fund. That's the good news. The bad news is that humans are involved.

3
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:22.880
Not all of it is available for allocation. First, the automatic deductions (think of these as the "cost of convincing humans

4
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:31.360
to stop killing each other" tax): Ten percent of the fund, or two point seven two billion dollars, goes to VICTORY Incentive

5
00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:39.620
Alignment Bond investors to repay campaign funders. Another ten percent, totaling two point seven two billion dollars, is

6
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:43.740
allocated for political incentives to keep politicians aligned.

7
00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:50.520
This leaves eighty percent, or twenty-one point eight billion dollars, available for Wishocracy to allocate.

8
00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:57.440
How do you stop parasitic special interests from stealing that twenty-one point eight billion dollars?

9
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:06.120
If history is any guide, you don't. Every pool of money humanity has ever created has attracted lobbyists the way a dropped

10
00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:10.100
ice cream cone attracts ants, except ants at least work hard.

11
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:16.040
Wishocracy replaces committees with code and representatives with mathematics.

12
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:23.700
It lets eight billion people collectively decide how to spend twenty-one point eight billion dollars without turning it

13
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:28.500
into a bureaucratic nightmare. The difference between this and the N.I.H.

14
00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:34.180
is like asking two hundred bureaucrats what you should have for lunch versus asking your stomach.

15
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:39.850
Your stomach has never once funded a colleague's lunch out of professional courtesy.

16
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:46.200
How Wishocracy Allocates the one percent Treaty Fund: Decentralized Crowdfunding.

17
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:53.000
Your decentralized institutes of health (D.I.H.) network functions as a decentralized crowdfunding platform.

18
00:01:54.380 --> 00:02:01.600
Anyone can submit a campaign proposal. Wishocracy allocates funds from the one percent Treaty Fund across competing campaigns.

19
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:07.180
It's Kickstarter, but backed by the G.D.P. of nations instead of your friend's credit card.

20
00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:17.120
What your D.F.D.A. (a decentralized F.D.A.) handles automatically (no voting required): Companies register and patients

21
00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.560
choose which treatments get tested (capitalism, for once, doing something useful).

22
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:29.620
Patients join trials for their conditions to determine which diseases get researched (people tend to be motivated about

23
00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:36.720
their own mortality). Market prices and participant choices handle resource allocation within trials.

24
00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:45.740
What Wishocracy Actually Decides: The hard stuff. Allocation across campaign proposals competing for one percent Treaty

25
00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:50.240
Fund funds: Infrastructure Campaigns (the boring but essential plumbing).

26
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.560
The Decentralized Framework for Drug Assessment Development is a one billion dollars per year proposal.

27
00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:02.760
The Epic electronic health records, or E.H.R., Integration Project is five million dollars one-time.

28
00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:10.100
Amazon Web Services, or A.W.S., Infrastructure Services costs three million dollars per year.

29
00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:14.640
The Security Audit Program costs two million dollars per year.

30
00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:22.100
An alternative D.F.D.A. (a decentralized F.D.A.) costs eight million dollars per year (competition!

31
00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:29.740
novel concept for government!). Public Goods (things the market won't fund because nobody gets rich curing diseases that

32
00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:37.260
don't sell pills). "Patient Trial Subsidies Program": eight hundred million dollars per year (automatic formula).

33
00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.820
"Off-Patent Drug Research": twenty billion dollars per year.

34
00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:50.020
"Rare Disease Initiative": ten billion dollars per year. "Negative Results Publishing Fund": five billion dollars per year

35
00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:58.760
(yes, knowing what *doesn't* work is worth billions; your species keeps re-running failed experiments because nobody publishes

36
00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:03.140
failures). Service Provider Bids (capitalism, but supervised).

37
00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:11.260
"Data Storage Provider A": two billion dollars per year. "Data Storage Provider B": one point five billion dollars per year

38
00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:17.480
(competing). "Pragmatic Clinical Trial Insurance Pool": ten billion dollar reserve.

39
00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:26.720
How It Works: Pairwise Comparisons Between Campaigns. No committee on Earth has ever looked at a list of ten thousand proposals

40
00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:31.620
and made a good decision. Committees are where good ideas go to die of old age.

41
00:04:32.540 --> 00:04:40.380
Instead, the global population votes using pairwise comparisons: "What's more important right now:" "Framework for drug

42
00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:46.480
assessment development" versus "Alzheimer's prize"? "Epic integration" versus "Security audits"?

43
00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:51.080
"Rare disease research" versus "An alternative drug assessment framework"?

44
00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:58.120
"Patient subsidies" versus "Infrastructure spending"? Millions of people make simple pairwise choices.

45
00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:01.940
The algorithm aggregates preferences into funding allocations.

46
00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:10.080
No filibustering. No horse-trading. No senators holding medical research hostage because they want a highway in their district.

47
00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:19.000
Why You Can't Just Use a Spreadsheet. Multiple competing implementations: Three teams want to build the drug assessment

48
00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:23.790
platform. Which one? All three? Two? The one with the best logo?

49
00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:31.700
Trade-offs between prizes: one hundred billion dollars for aging reversal or fifty billion dollars for Alzheimer's plus

50
00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:36.340
fifty billion dollars for cancer? There is no "correct" answer.

51
00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:42.260
There is only what humanity collectively values, which is exactly what Wishocracy measures.

52
00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:49.040
Public goods allocation: How much for off-patent drugs versus rare diseases?

53
00:05:49.580 --> 00:05:57.200
Markets won't answer this because there's no profit in it. Service provider competition: Which companies get contracts for

54
00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:04.940
what amounts? Your decentralized F.D.A. allocates resources WITHIN pragmatic clinical trials.

55
00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:12.240
Wishocracy allocates resources BETWEEN campaigns. One is the engine, the other is the steering wheel.

56
00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:21.160
You need both unless you enjoy driving into walls. The core of Wishocracy is a voting method so simple, even a congressman

57
00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:30.020
could do it (though we'd recommend they practice first). It's called Aggregated Pairwise Preference Allocation, or simply,

58
00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:35.910
the Pairwise Slider Allocation. Your brain can't rank a list of twenty priorities.

59
00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:42.020
It gives up around item number seven. But it's fantastic at comparing two things.

60
00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:50.720
Instead of asking, "Rank these ten thousand campaign proposals," the system asks: "What's more important right now: decentralized

61
00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:55.680
F.D.A. protocol development or security audits?" You make a choice.

62
00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:59.320
That's it. You do this a few times with different random pairs.

63
00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:05.320
It takes five minutes. Less time than you spend choosing a Netflix show, and considerably more important.

64
00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:12.960
Millions of other people do the same. An algorithm takes all these simple, head-to-head comparisons and builds a real-time

65
00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:19.560
ranking of infrastructure and public goods priorities. (Remember: Wishocracy doesn't allocate between diseases.

66
00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:27.300
Patients do that by choosing which trials to join. Wishocracy allocates between *infrastructure campaigns* and *public goods*

67
00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:33.640
that markets can't handle.). It's the wisdom of crowds, but without the crowds having to talk to each other.

68
00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:41.780
Why This Actually Works (Math Warning). When millions of people make pairwise choices, something almost magical happens

69
00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:48.760
(and by "magical" I mean "statistically inevitable," which is the only kind of magic that actually works): Random pairs

70
00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:53.780
prevent gaming. You can't lobby five million people. Well, you can try.

71
00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:01.820
Good luck. Statistical models (Bradley-Terry, PageRank) extract global preferences from individual comparisons.

72
00:08:02.520 --> 00:08:06.760
The same math that ranks Google search results can rank humanity's medical priorities.

73
00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:10.760
Google uses it to find cat videos. You'll use it to cure cancer.

74
00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:19.620
Progress. Outliers cancel out. The guy who votes "fund my personal jetpack" gets drowned out by the millions voting for

75
00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:24.700
"cure my mother's Alzheimer's.". Wisdom of crowds emerges. Reliably.

76
00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:30.240
Repeatedly. Boringly. Example with real numbers: Five million people vote.

77
00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:34.760
Each makes twenty comparisons. That's one hundred million data points.

78
00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:43.179
Algorithm processes them in seconds. Output: "Allocate forty percent to patient subsidy programs, twenty-five percent to

79
00:08:43.299 --> 00:08:51.040
decentralized F.D.A. protocol development, fifteen percent to off-patent drug research, ten percent to rare disease initiatives,

80
00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:55.810
five percent to security infrastructure, five percent to negative results publishing...".

81
00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:58.700
No committee meetings were harmed in the making of this budget.

82
00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:05.820
It's democracy without the shouting. The stupidity is still there, but it's evenly distributed across millions of people

83
00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:10.060
and thus cancels out. This is called "the wisdom of crowds."

84
00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:17.480
It works for the same reason that guessing the weight of a cow at a fair works: individually you're all terrible at it,

85
00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:23.480
but collectively you're weirdly accurate. From Priorities to Projects (Where Wishes Become Tasks).

86
00:09:24.820 --> 00:09:30.160
Great, so the Pairwise Slider Allocation tells you "Curing Alzheimer's" is a top priority.

87
00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:34.850
Wonderful. Now what? Having a priority without a plan is just a wish.

88
00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:40.340
And wishes, as a rule, don't cure anything. Wishocracy translates that priority into action.

89
00:09:41.140 --> 00:09:50.370
One. A.I. Breaks It Down: An A.I. takes the impossible goal of "Cure Alzheimer's" and breaks it into thousands of smaller,

90
00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:56.020
concrete, fundable tasks. "Cure Alzheimer's" becomes "Map protein structures."

91
00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:03.020
That becomes "Run AlphaFold on these sequences." That becomes "Rent computing time."

92
00:10:03.960 --> 00:10:09.520
Every impossible problem is just a series of possible steps arranged in a line.

93
00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:17.300
Your species figured this out for building pyramids four thousand five hundred years ago but keeps forgetting to apply it

94
00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:23.740
to medicine. Two. The Bounty Board: The system posts these tasks to a global marketplace.

95
00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:29.640
It's like eBay, but for saving humanity. WANTED: A cure for Alzheimer's.

96
00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:36.280
BOUNTY: ten billion dollars. WANTED: A map of all protein misfolding patterns.

97
00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:45.640
BOUNTY: five hundred million dollars. Three. The World Competes: The best teams from around the world, from M.I.T.

98
00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:52.780
to some kid in a garage in Lagos, bid on these tasks. The system funds multiple approaches in parallel.

99
00:10:53.360 --> 00:10:58.210
The ones that show promise get more funding. The ones that fail lose funding instantly.

100
00:10:59.080 --> 00:11:07.340
It's venture capital, but for not dying. Your species already uses this method to decide which restaurants survive and which

101
00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:11.640
phone apps get downloaded. Time to try it with the thing that kills you.
